Dear ICLR PC, SAC, AC and all reviewers,

We are deeply grateful for the efforts of the chairs and for the reviewers’ thoughtful and constructive feedback. We greatly appreciate your recognition of the importance of reusing low-quality instruction data, the contribution of constructing a new dataset tailored to this problem, and the empirical strength and reproducibility of our pipeline. Your comments have helped us substantially improve the clarity, positioning, and completeness of this work.

Across the reviews, the main concerns focus on the following aspects:
(1) the motivation and conceptual novelty of our proposed instruction distillation paradigm relative to existing data augmentation and data selection methods;
(2) the clarity of data construction and experimental configurations (e.g., the precise meaning of “MIXUP 50% + ORI 50%”);
(3) the potential bias of LLM-based rating and the interpretation of different low-/high-quality mixture ratios; and
(4) the need for comparisons with simpler or more established baselines, such as direct LLM rewriting, high-quality-only training, and traditional data augmentation methods.

In the revised version, we have made the following improvements:
(1) further clarified the practical applicability and motivation of our method (see Section 1);
(2) clearly distinguished instruction distillation from data augmentation and data curation, and elaborated the conceptual differences (see Appendix B);
(3) substantially expanded the description of our data scoring, sampling, and mixing pipeline, and moved the full protocol to the appendix for reproducibility (see Appendix E and F);
(4) added new baseline experiments, including back-translation, 4o-mini paraphrasing, EDA, and simple LLM rewriting using the same base model (see Section 5.2);
(5) provided a more detailed analysis of rating variance and mixture-ratio behavior, together with additional experimental results and clearer explanations to avoid misinterpretation (see Appendix F);
(6) revised several passages to improve clarity and avoid ambiguous or overly technical expressions (e.g., Section 3.1), and further expanded the discussion of related work.

We hope these clarifications and additions adequately address the reviewers’ concerns, and we sincerely appreciate your efforts in helping us improve the paper.

Best regards,

Authors
